+### Why pending-request readiness is its own flag and not the bootstrap status
+
+A directory store reaches `status: "complete"` only when every critical read resolved, and that set includes the project config and location, which say nothing about pending requests. Gating the badge on it meant a project whose config read failed — a supported state, with its own `ProjectConfigErrorToast` — never trusted its store, so the additive index kept showing requests the store had already cleared, and a failed re-bootstrap of a complete directory dropped the store's authority the same way. `pendingRequestsReady` commits when both request snapshots do, so an unrelated failed read cannot decide a badge. It is never cleared afterwards: the committed snapshot stays the better answer than the index while a new read is in flight or after it fails.
+
+### Why a local clear settles the cross-directory index
+
+The index was written only by lifecycle events and the host seed, so a reply whose settlement event fell into an SSE gap left its entry there while the store cleared. Every surface that reads only the index then offered a request no row showed. `settleGlobalBlockingRequest` is the one path a local clear takes into the index, called from both store-removal helpers, so all consumers keep reading one answer. Reconciling the index whenever a store became authoritative was the alternative: it would have needed a directory-wide diff on every readiness change, and it would still miss a clear that happens while a store is already ready.
